So, in our standard logic library which

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.590
we use standard logic 11 IEEE standard

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.310
logic 1164, there are no arithmetic

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.510
operations defined.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.310
So, the package numeric standard

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.270
from this from this library

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.230
defines two vector types whose elements

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.430
are also of type standard logic but from

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.670
which we can do numeric numerical

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.670
calculations, arithmetic.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.390
So, if you if you include the numeric

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.950
library so down here here is are the

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.670
definitions. So, library IEEE

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.270
use IEEE standard logic 1164 all

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.950
basically gives us standard logic

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.630
standard logic vector

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.750
and and the values that those can take.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.070
So, that gives us our bit

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.670
representations and a vector of bits and

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.950
then we can also use the IEEE numeric

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.470
standard and that will give us two

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.070
additional types that allow us to do

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.590
arithmetic. And these are unsigned

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.150
values.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.630
So, that's an unsigned binary number

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.430
with where the right um

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:43.950
and actually I'm going to talk a little

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.310
bit more about order of bits in a

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.230
minute. And then we also have the signed

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.070
value as well. So, we can have signed

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.670
arithmetic or unsigned arithmetic using

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.710
these two types, right? Which are

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.670
defined in this numeric library. And

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.550
these are what we should be using.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.550
Um just a little bit about signed

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.190
numbers. I'm I'm I'm assuming you've

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.190
actually done this already. So, we have

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.790
a fixed width binary representation, and

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.350
we want to represent both positive and

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.310
negative numbers. So, the idea and we

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.590
one of the things we want is they wrap

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.830
around under addition and subtraction as

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.830
all operations are mod 2 to the power n

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.990
because n is the number of bits.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.070
So, what we use is what's called two's

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:22.790
complement notation. I'm not going to go

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.190
through that in detail, but that's what

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.949
we what the IEEE library uses and what

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:28.949
we normally would use.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:30.630
And basically, it's defined as the

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.390
complement with respect to 2n. What that

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.949
would to do the So, a negative number

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:36.949
here we've got uh one

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:40.910
a value of one is just the bits 0001.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:41.776
Um

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:44.150
So, what if uh right? So, if we want the

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.670
two's complement, the first thing is for

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.470
a negative number, the most significant

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.630
bit is one.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.830
Uh and we invert the digits and add one.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.590
So, minus one

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.550
right? If we So, we basically uh we as I

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.310
said, if we invert all of the digits

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.949
0001, we would have 1110 and then add

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:06.110
one and it gives us 1111. So, if you

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.270
think about it, that's one below zero if

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.550
they wrap around in a circle.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.990
We go zero the whole way around to 1111.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.030
So, it's it's if you go anti-clock

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.750
anti-clockwise, it would go right. So,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.630
this is basically what two's complement

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.670
means. So, that's how we're representing

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.390
binary numbers and such that when we do

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.590
addition and subtraction, it does modulo

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.710
2n correctly.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.590
I just wanted to mention that. So, we've

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.390
got signed and unsigned types that you

